<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shkodër</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Scutari, Scodra, Skadar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shkodër is a city in north-western Albania on the south-eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Shkodër</a> and one of the oldest towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. As Scodra it was the capital of the Illyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentius">Gentius</a>, captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 168 BCE. It later belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1479, and it long remained a centre of Albanian Catholic and national culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shkoder.jpg" alt="Shkodër" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivan Koev · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_City_and_the_Prokletije_from_the_castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
